IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`
IMPORT `ccc1-support`

-- ccc1-s21-24-parties.l4 -- Part I (General), sections 21 to 24: parties to offences (aiding, abetting, common intention, counselling),
-- organizations (negligence, and other fault), accessory after the fact, and attempts.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30).
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- Each function is named for what ITS SUBSECTION does ("the subsection makes the person a party"), because another section may also make the person a party:
-- JUST FALSE is "this subsection does not", never "the person is not a party". The punishment of the offence a party aided, abetted or attempted is the answer of the part that
-- owns the offence (PLAN.md seam R2) and is not asked here: these sections decide whether the person is a party or guilty of an attempt, not how much they are punished.
-- A "who" description that is not met answers NOTHING; a "Where" or "if" gate that is not met answers JUST FALSE.

§ `Section 21 -- Parties to offence`

-- src:587 | Parties to Offences
-- src:588 | Parties to offence
-- src:589 | 21 (1) Every one is a party to an offence who
-- src:590 | (a) actually commits it;
-- src:591 | (b) does or omits to do anything for the purpose of aiding any person to commit it; or
-- src:592 | (c) abets any person in committing it.
@ref s 21(1) (src:587-592)
GIVEN `the person actually commits it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person does or omits to do anything for the purpose of aiding any person to commit it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person abets any person in committing it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 21(1) the subsection makes the person a party to the offence` MEANS
    IF `the person actually commits it` OR `the person does or omits to do anything for the purpose of aiding any person to commit it` OR `the person abets any person in committing it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "Every one is a party to an offence who" names three ways; subsection 21(2) and sections 22, 22.1 and 22.2 make others parties

-- src:593 | Common intention
-- src:594 | (2) Where two or more persons form an intention in common to carry out an unlawful purpose and to assist each other therein and any one of them, in carrying out the common purpose, commits an offence, each of them who knew or ought to have known that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose is a party to that offence.
@ref s 21(2) (src:593-594)
GIVEN `two or more persons form an intention in common to carry out an unlawful purpose and to assist each other therein` IS A BOOLEAN
      `any one of them, in carrying out the common purpose, commits an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person knew that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person ought to have known that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 21(2) the subsection makes the person a party to the offence committed in carrying out the common purpose` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`two or more persons form an intention in common to carry out an unlawful purpose and to assist each other therein` AND `any one of them, in carrying out the common purpose, commits an offence`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where two or more persons form an intention in common to carry out an unlawful purpose and to assist each other therein and any one of them, in carrying out the common purpose, commits an offence"
        IF `the person knew that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose` OR `the person ought to have known that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "each of them who knew or ought to have known that the commission of the offence would be a probable consequence of carrying out the common purpose"

§ `Section 22 -- Person counselling offence`

-- src:595 | Person counselling offence
-- src:596 | 22 (1) Where a person counsels another person to be a party to an offence and that other person is afterwards a party to that offence, the person who counselled is a party to that offence, notwithstanding that the offence was committed in a way different from that which was counselled.
@ref s 22(1) (src:595-596)
GIVEN `the person counsels another person to be a party to an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that other person is afterwards a party to that offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence was committed in a way different from that which was counselled` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22(1) the subsection makes the person who counselled a party to the offence` MEANS
    IF `the person counsels another person to be a party to an offence` AND `that other person is afterwards a party to that offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE   -- whether or not `the offence was committed in a way different from that which was counselled`: "notwithstanding that the offence was committed in a way different from that which was counselled"
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a person counsels another person to be a party to an offence and that other person is afterwards a party to that offence"

-- src:597 | Idem
-- src:598 | (2) Every one who counsels another person to be a party to an offence is a party to every offence that the other commits in consequence of the counselling that the person who counselled knew or ought to have known was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling.
@ref s 22(2) (src:597-598)
GIVEN `the person counsels another person to be a party to an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other person commits the offence in consequence of the counselling` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person who counselled knew that the offence was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person who counselled ought to have known that the offence was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22(2) the subsection makes the person who counselled a party to the offence the other commits` MEANS
    IF `the person counsels another person to be a party to an offence` AND `the other person commits the offence in consequence of the counselling`
       AND (`the person who counselled knew that the offence was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling` OR `the person who counselled ought to have known that the offence was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "every offence that the other commits in consequence of the counselling that the person who counselled knew or ought to have known was likely to be committed in consequence of the counselling"

-- src:599 | Definition of counsel
-- src:600 | (3) For the purposes of this Act, counsel includes procure, solicit or incite.
@ref s 22(3) (src:599-600)
DECLARE `s 22(3) the conduct` IS ONE OF
    `s 22(3) counselling in its ordinary sense`
    `s 22(3) procuring`
    `s 22(3) soliciting`
    `s 22(3) inciting`
    `s 22(3) conduct that is none of those`

@ref s 22(3) (src:599-600)
GIVEN conduct IS A `s 22(3) the conduct`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22(3) the conduct is counselling for the purposes of this Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER conduct
    WHEN `s 22(3) counselling in its ordinary sense` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 22(3) procuring` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 22(3) soliciting` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 22(3) inciting` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 22(3) conduct that is none of those` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "counsel includes procure, solicit or incite"

§ `Sections 22.1 and 22.2 -- Organizations`

-- src:601 | Offences of negligence — organizations
-- src:602 | 22.1 In respect of an offence that requires the prosecution to prove negligence, an organization is a party to the offence if
-- src:603 | (a) acting within the scope of their authority
-- src:604 | (i) one of its representatives is a party to the offence, or
-- src:605 | (ii) two or more of its representatives engage in conduct, whether by act or omission, such that, if it had been the conduct of only one representative, that representative would have been a party to the offence; and
-- src:606 | (b) the senior officer who is responsible for the aspect of the organization’s activities that is relevant to the offence departs — or the senior officers, collectively, depart — markedly from the standard of care that, in the circumstances, could reasonably be expected to prevent a representative of the organization from being a party to the offence.
@ref s 22.1 (src:601-606)
GIVEN `the offence requires the prosecution to prove negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 2 organization: the accused is one` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 2 representative: the person acting is a representative of the organization` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 2 senior officer: the officer responsible for the aspect of the activities, or the officers collectively, are senior officers` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `acting within the scope of their authority, one of its representatives is a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `acting within the scope of their authority, two or more of its representatives engage in conduct, whether by act or omission, such that, if it had been the conduct of only one representative, that representative would have been a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the senior officer who is responsible for the aspect of the organization's activities that is relevant to the offence departs markedly from the standard of care that, in the circumstances, could reasonably be expected to prevent a representative of the organization from being a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the senior officers, collectively, depart markedly from that standard of care` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22.1 the section makes the organization a party to the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the offence requires the prosecution to prove negligence` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In respect of an offence that requires the prosecution to prove negligence"
        IF `s 2 organization: the accused is one` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the accused is an organization is the answer of section 2, which was not given
        IF `s 2 organization: the accused is one` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "an organization is a party to the offence"
        IF `the conditions` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the conditions` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an organization is a party to the offence if"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the person acting is a representative, or the officers are senior officers, is the answer of section 2, which was not given
    WHERE
        `paragraph (a)` MEANS
            `ccc1 both of` `s 2 representative: the person acting is a representative of the organization`
                (`ccc1 a fact as an answer` (`acting within the scope of their authority, one of its representatives is a party to the offence` OR `acting within the scope of their authority, two or more of its representatives engage in conduct, whether by act or omission, such that, if it had been the conduct of only one representative, that representative would have been a party to the offence`))
        `paragraph (b)` MEANS
            `ccc1 both of` `s 2 senior officer: the officer responsible for the aspect of the activities, or the officers collectively, are senior officers`
                (`ccc1 a fact as an answer` (`the senior officer who is responsible for the aspect of the organization's activities that is relevant to the offence departs markedly from the standard of care that, in the circumstances, could reasonably be expected to prevent a representative of the organization from being a party to the offence` OR `the senior officers, collectively, depart markedly from that standard of care`))
        `the conditions` MEANS `ccc1 both of` `paragraph (a)` `paragraph (b)`

-- src:607 | Other offences — organizations
-- src:608 | 22.2 In respect of an offence that requires the prosecution to prove fault — other than negligence — an organization is a party to the offence if, with the intent at least in part to benefit the organization, one of its senior officers
-- src:609 | (a) acting within the scope of their authority, is a party to the offence;
-- src:610 | (b) having the mental state required to be a party to the offence and acting within the scope of their authority, directs the work of other representatives of the organization so that they do the act or make the omission specified in the offence; or
-- src:611 | (c) knowing that a representative of the organization is or is about to be a party to the offence, does not take all reasonable measures to stop them from being a party to the offence.
@ref s 22.2 (src:607-611)
GIVEN `the offence requires the prosecution to prove fault other than negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 2 organization: the accused is one` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 2 senior officer: the person acting is one of the organization's senior officers` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the senior officer acts with the intent at least in part to benefit the organization` IS A BOOLEAN
      `acting within the scope of their authority, the senior officer is a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `having the mental state required to be a party to the offence and acting within the scope of their authority, the senior officer directs the work of other representatives of the organization so that they do the act or make the omission specified in the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `knowing that a representative of the organization is or is about to be a party to the offence, the senior officer does not take all reasonable measures to stop them from being a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22.2 the section makes the organization a party to the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the offence requires the prosecution to prove fault other than negligence` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In respect of an offence that requires the prosecution to prove fault — other than negligence"
        IF `s 2 organization: the accused is one` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the accused is an organization is the answer of section 2, which was not given
        IF `s 2 organization: the accused is one` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "an organization is a party to the offence"
        IF `the conditions` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the conditions` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an organization is a party to the offence if, with the intent at least in part to benefit the organization, one of its senior officers"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the person acting is a senior officer is the answer of section 2, which was not given
    WHERE
        `the conditions` MEANS
            `ccc1 both of` `s 2 senior officer: the person acting is one of the organization's senior officers`
                (`ccc1 a fact as an answer` (`the senior officer acts with the intent at least in part to benefit the organization`
                    AND (`acting within the scope of their authority, the senior officer is a party to the offence`
                         OR `having the mental state required to be a party to the offence and acting within the scope of their authority, the senior officer directs the work of other representatives of the organization so that they do the act or make the omission specified in the offence`
                         OR `knowing that a representative of the organization is or is about to be a party to the offence, the senior officer does not take all reasonable measures to stop them from being a party to the offence`)))

§ `Section 23 and 23.1 -- Accessory after the fact`

-- src:612 | Accessory after the fact
-- src:613 | 23 (1) An accessory after the fact to an offence is one who, knowing that a person has been a party to the offence, receives, comforts or assists that person for the purpose of enabling that person to escape.
@ref s 23(1) (src:612-613)
GIVEN `the person knows that another person has been a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person receives, comforts or assists that person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person does so for the purpose of enabling that person to escape` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 23(1) the person is an accessory after the fact to the offence` MEANS
    IF `the person knows that another person has been a party to the offence` AND `the person receives, comforts or assists that person` AND `the person does so for the purpose of enabling that person to escape`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An accessory after the fact to an offence is one who, knowing that a person has been a party to the offence, receives, comforts or assists that person for the purpose of enabling that person to escape."

-- src:614 | (2) [Repealed, 2000, c. 12, s. 92]
@ref s 23(2) (src:614-614)
`s 23(2) as the text shows it` MEANS "[Repealed, 2000, c. 12, s. 92]"

-- src:615 | Where one party cannot be convicted
-- src:616 | 23.1 For greater certainty, sections 21 to 23 apply in respect of an accused notwithstanding the fact that the person whom the accused aids or abets, counsels or procures or receives, comforts or assists cannot be convicted of the offence.
@ref s 23.1 (src:615-616)
GIVEN `the person whom the accused aids or abets, counsels or procures or receives, comforts or assists cannot be convicted of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 23.1 sections 21 to 23 apply in respect of the accused although the other person cannot be convicted` MEANS
    IF `the person whom the accused aids or abets, counsels or procures or receives, comforts or assists cannot be convicted of the offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "notwithstanding the fact that the person whom the accused aids or abets, counsels or procures or receives, comforts or assists cannot be convicted of the offence"

§ `Section 24 -- Attempts`

-- src:617 | Attempts
-- src:618 | 24 (1) Every one who, having an intent to commit an offence, does or omits to do anything for the purpose of carrying out the intention is guilty of an attempt to commit the offence whether or not it was possible under the circumstances to commit the offence.
@ref s 24(1) (src:617-618)
GIVEN `the person has an intent to commit an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person does or omits to do anything for the purpose of carrying out the intention` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was possible under the circumstances to commit the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 24(1) the person is guilty of an attempt to commit the offence` MEANS
    IF `the person has an intent to commit an offence` AND `the person does or omits to do anything for the purpose of carrying out the intention`
    THEN JUST TRUE   -- whether or not `it was possible under the circumstances to commit the offence`
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Every one who, having an intent to commit an offence, does or omits to do anything for the purpose of carrying out the intention"

-- src:619 | Question of law
-- src:620 | (2) The question whether an act or omission by a person who has an intent to commit an offence is or is not mere preparation to commit the offence, and too remote to constitute an attempt to commit the offence, is a question of law.
@ref s 24(2) (src:619-620)
GIVEN `the question is whether an act or omission by a person who has an intent to commit an offence is or is not mere preparation to commit the offence, and too remote to constitute an attempt to commit the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 24(2) the question is a question of law` MEANS
    IF `the question is whether an act or omission by a person who has an intent to commit an offence is or is not mere preparation to commit the offence, and too remote to constitute an attempt to commit the offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The question whether an act or omission by a person who has an intent to commit an offence is or is not mere preparation to commit the offence"
